WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.199
Is your helmet on tight? Is your harness buckled?

2
00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:09.328
Are you feeling courageous? Well then, let's fly!

3
00:00:09.329 --> 00:00:13.464
Today Josh and Anne are taking us on an adventure through the skies.

4
00:00:13.465 --> 00:00:18.966
With no motor, no parachute, and no hot air balloon, they are going flying.

5
00:00:18.967 --> 00:00:21.550
How? With hang gliders!

6
00:00:21.551 --> 00:00:29.070
Josh and Anne are joining us today in Queenstown, New Zealand, a city whose people are known for having fun in the great outdoors.

7
00:00:29.071 --> 00:00:34.015
"I have always wanted to fly, and I think hang gliding sounds like fun.

8
00:00:34.016 --> 00:00:36.484
Do you want to come?" says Josh.

9
00:00:36.485 --> 00:00:40.201
"It sounds exciting, but I'm a little nervous about it.

10
00:00:40.202 --> 00:00:46.061
Then again, it's good to try new things . . . so let's go!" says Anne.

11
00:00:46.062 --> 00:00:48.823
Hang gliding is actually very easy.

12
00:00:48.824 --> 00:00:52.034
Josh will fly with a championship glider named Tom.

13
00:00:52.035 --> 00:00:55.367
Tom is great at doing stunts and flying fast.

14
00:00:55.368 --> 00:01:02.665
Anne will fly with a glide instructor named Rick, who is good at teaching beginners.

15
00:01:02.666 --> 00:01:09.725
With the hang gliders packed on the roof of the van, everyone drives to the top of the mountain that overlooks Queenstown.

16
00:01:09.726 --> 00:01:15.176
It takes the instructors only twenty minutes to assemble poles and pieces into a complete glider.

17
00:01:15.177 --> 00:01:19.068
Josh and Anne seem pretty impressed.

18
00:01:19.069 --> 00:01:22.352
Rick calls Anne over for her first lesson on flying.

19
00:01:22.353 --> 00:01:26.033
"Okay, first, you need a helmet that fits your head well.

20
00:01:26.034 --> 00:01:27.621
How does this one work?"

21
00:01:27.622 --> 00:01:32.859
"It feels comfortable and nice and hard," says Anne.

22
00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:35.678
Then Rick explains the next piece of equipment.

23
00:01:35.679 --> 00:01:39.800
"Great. Next you need a harness. This one should fit you.

24
00:01:39.801 --> 00:01:43.355
It looks like a sleeping bag, but it is very strong.

25
00:01:43.356 --> 00:01:47.650
It will hook you to the glider tightly so you can't fall out."

26
00:01:47.651 --> 00:01:51.400
"I'd better not fall out!"

27
00:01:51.401 --> 00:01:56.242
While Rick and Anne get her harness fitted, Tom gets Josh ready for their jump.

28
00:01:56.243 --> 00:02:00.842
"Cheers, Josh. When we're hooked up to the glider, I'll ask if you're ready.

29
00:02:00.843 --> 00:02:04.412
If you feel nervous and you're not ready, say 'Wait.'

30
00:02:04.413 --> 00:02:07.561
But, if you feel ready, say 'Ready.' Got it?"

31
00:02:07.562 --> 00:02:12.753
"Excellent! I'm ready to fly already! Let's go!" says Josh.

32
00:02:12.754 --> 00:02:16.553
"In just a minute, we'll start back here, away from the edge.

33
00:02:16.554 --> 00:02:22.650
When you say ready, I'll count one, two, three. At one, we start running.

34
00:02:22.651 --> 00:02:27.163
At two, we run harder. At three, we throw ourselves off the cliff."

35
00:02:27.164 --> 00:02:31.347
"I throw myself off the cliff?" Josh is worried.

36
00:02:31.348 --> 00:02:35.505
"Don't worry," explains Tom. "Your wings will be attached.

37
00:02:35.506 --> 00:02:39.899
It's important that you run faster and faster as I count from one to three.

38
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:45.245
When we run fast off the edge, it gives our glider momentum and pushes us out.

39
00:02:45.246 --> 00:02:49.259
But if we go slowly, we'll fall straight down. Got it?"

40
00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:52.729
"I got it. No worries."

41
00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:55.577
Running in a harness is more difficult than it looks.

42
00:02:55.578 --> 00:02:58.507
It's like running with a sleeping bag around your legs.

43
00:02:58.508 --> 00:03:02.882
While Josh practices running, Rick teaches Anne how to steer.

44
00:03:02.883 --> 00:03:05.641
"Turning the glider is really simple, Anne.

45
00:03:05.642 --> 00:03:10.081
There is a triangular bar in front that is attached to the top of the glider.

46
00:03:10.082 --> 00:03:13.496
If we want to go left, we push the bar to the right.

47
00:03:13.497 --> 00:03:16.714
If we want to go up, we push the bar forward.

48
00:03:16.715 --> 00:03:21.495
And pulling the bar towards us sends us down. But I don't recommend that."

49
00:03:21.496 --> 00:03:26.165
"I think I'll let you do the steering, thanks," says Anne.

50
00:03:26.166 --> 00:03:30.644
Once everyone has practiced, it's time to get strapped into the gliders.

51
00:03:30.645 --> 00:03:38.823
Anne reminds herself it's good to try new things, and Josh reminds himself of the one-two-three. Josh and Tom go first.

52
00:03:38.824 --> 00:03:39.693
"Ready?"

53
00:03:39.694 --> 00:03:40.836
"Ready!"

54
00:03:40.837 --> 00:03:44.320
"One . . . two . . . three . . ."

55
00:03:44.321 --> 00:03:50.671
And they're off! Anne and Rick are up next, and soon they are flying right above Josh and Tom.

56
00:03:50.672 --> 00:03:55.920
While Josh and Tom are diving and flying sideways, Anne and Rick take in the view.

57
00:03:55.921 --> 00:04:01.774
Hang gliding is a nature-friendly sport because there is no noise and no pollution.

58
00:04:01.775 --> 00:04:05.931
Both gliders circle the fields below. And it's a perfect landing!

59
00:04:05.932 --> 00:04:09.762
"That was the most beautiful flight I could have imagined!

60
00:04:09.763 --> 00:04:15.072
It was so peaceful, I actually felt natural up there!" says Anne.

61
00:04:15.073 --> 00:04:18.916
Josh agrees, but with a different sort of enthusiasm.

62
00:04:18.917 --> 00:04:21.351
"Woo-hoo! That was amazing!

63
00:04:21.352 --> 00:04:25.116
We flew sideways and did all sorts of dives and turns.

64
00:04:25.117 --> 00:04:30.345
It was absolutely awesome. Wow! I can't wait to do that again."

65
00:04:30.346 --> 00:04:34.905
This concludes another short but thrilling adventure with Josh and Anne.

66
00:04:34.906 --> 00:04:39.326
Who knows where their next adventure may take us! Bye!

